WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.669
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:04.837
and Emmy Award-nominated actor
you know from films such as

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:06.972
"Closer," "Sin City,"
and "Children of Men"

00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:08.241
and shows like "The Knick"

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:10.210
and "A Murder
at the End of the World."

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.813
He stars in "Monsieur Spade,"
which airs Sunday nights on AMC.

00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:14.914
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:19.352
-Maybe you forgot you were
going to stay off my property.

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.086
-Your property.
-Oh, Jesus.

00:00:21.086 --> 00:00:23.222
Have you been
sitting there since last night?

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:24.958
-What are you talking about?
I wasn't here last night.

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:26.125
-Then who was?
-How would I know?

00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:27.393
I wasn't here.

00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:30.296
-Philippe Saint Andre --
did you lend him your bike?

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:31.998
-Sometimes, he just takes it.

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.134
He's an old friend, after all.
-Oh.

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:36.536
With friends like that,
who needs friends?

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:43.008
Jean-Pierre, there's some things
you just don't ever want to do.

00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:45.712
-Please welcome back to
the show Clive Owen, everybody.

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:48.348
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:00.092 --> 00:01:03.696
-Welcome back, Clive.
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:05.531
-Oh, I'm happy to be here.

00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:08.067
-You were last here in 2015,
which is a while ago,

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.501
and we were talking
about your beloved

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:10.603
football club, Liverpool.

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:11.637
-Yes.

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:13.239
-They weren't doing
too well in 2015,

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.774
but, you know,
not bad this year.

00:01:15.774 --> 00:01:17.910
-Better than not bad.
Top of the league.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.513
I think we're in with a shot.
-Alright. Very exciting.

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.081
Do you ever get a chance
to go to games anymore?

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:23.983
-I do. Yeah, yeah.
I go when I can, yeah.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:26.185
-Alright. And do they --
Are they excited to see you

00:01:26.185 --> 00:01:27.553
or they just care
what's on the pitch

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:28.754
and they don't care
about you at all?

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:29.889
-No, they don't
care about me at all.

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.824
-Okay, that's good.
Helps keep you grounded.

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:33.659
-Yeah.
-This is very --

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:35.962
This is a really cool project.
It's a very cool idea.

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:39.432
This is the very famous
film noir detective Sam Spade,

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.367
famously played in
"The Maltese Falcon"

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:42.969
by Humphrey Bogart.

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:45.305
But this is a different
part of his life.

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:49.508
-Yeah. So, what we've done is,
we've jumped 20 years ahead.

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:51.711
He's living in
the South of France.

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:54.980
And you have some flashbacks
of how he ended up there

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.850
and what the situation is.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.986
He's trying to live the quiet
life in the South of France,

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:01.988
and then, at the end
of the first episode,

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.789
we unearth something terrible
has happened,

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:07.159
and the old Sam Spade's
going to have to come back out.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:09.429
-That's always the way
with these retired detectives.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.931
Something always brings you out.

00:02:11.931 --> 00:02:14.667
I read a really funny --
a comment you had

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:15.935
in a profile of Scott Frank,

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:18.170
who's the wonderful writer
who created the show,

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.173
about how he sold you
on the idea of playing

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:21.608
Sam Spade,
which is so exciting,

00:02:21.608 --> 00:02:23.642
and then maybe left out that
it was 20 years in the future.

00:02:23.642 --> 00:02:24.844
-20 years in the future,

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:26.679
took away the hat,
took away the mac,

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:29.149
took away the smoking,
took away the gun.

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:31.951
So my running gag
with him was, "Duped.

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:33.419
I've been duped."

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:36.055
Like, I need to go off and find
another noir project to do,

00:02:36.055 --> 00:02:37.757
because this isn't the one.

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.826
-Now, I will say,
one way he didn't dupe you --

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:40.926
South of France.

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:43.229
-Yeah.
-You were also wonderful in

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:44.363
"A Murder at
the End of the World."

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:45.697
That's Iceland.

00:02:45.697 --> 00:02:47.633
I feel like you deserved
a nice South of France project.

00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:49.835
-Oh, it's true, to go from,
yeah, the very cold

00:02:49.835 --> 00:02:51.303
to the lovely South of France.

00:02:51.303 --> 00:02:52.939
-Yeah. Well,
it's a beautiful show,

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:54.173
it looks beautiful,
And I would assume it was

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:55.407
as nice to be there
as it looks on camera.

00:02:55.407 --> 00:02:58.177
-It was fantastic.
Yeah, it was really idyllic.

00:02:58.177 --> 00:02:59.311
It was beautiful.

00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:01.481
-Was that a movie --
"Maltese Falcon" --

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:03.349
I mean, obviously, neither of us
were around when it came out,

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.718
but I remember where I found it
in a video store.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.654
What was your first experience
with that movie?

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:08.954
-I'm crazy about that movie.

00:03:08.954 --> 00:03:10.889
I actually have an original
poster of that movie.

00:03:10.889 --> 00:03:12.325
Like, I adore that movie.

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:14.526
And I would have
seen that in the --

00:03:14.526 --> 00:03:16.962
I was at drama school
in the early '80s,

00:03:16.962 --> 00:03:18.865
and there was about
three or four

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:22.035
great rep cinemas that showed,
like, regularly,

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:24.403
old, classic movies,
and I spent a lot of time

00:03:24.403 --> 00:03:26.572
going and catching up
with the classics.

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:28.073
-When you play a character
like that

00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:30.676
that was so iconically played
by Humphrey Bogart,

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:32.278
like, do you try to remember it

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:34.814
or try to forget about it
when you're on set?

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:37.049
-I think, you know, what
you're supposed to do is go,

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:38.450
"I'm going to go
and put my stamp on it

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.819
and do my interpretation,"

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:43.889
but I am such a Bogart fan
that I used it as an opportunity

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:46.892
to go drink him in
and lean right into that,

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:49.895
because although
we've jumped 20 years ahead,

00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:52.532
the origins of him
is that San Francisco

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:55.734
private detective from the '40s,
and I wanted that flavor

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:58.203
to still be sort of simmering
through everything.

00:03:58.203 --> 00:03:59.906
-That's a really fun thing
to be able to bring to a role,

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:02.608
like, just to not just research
the character on the page,

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:04.076
but also know that there
was also this performance

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:05.444
that you can pull from.

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:08.281
-I adore Bogart, and, like,
you know, it's the cliché,

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.482
but "Casablanca" is my
all-time favorite movie.

00:04:10.482 --> 00:04:13.652
-It's kind of a perfect movie.
It does not age badly.

00:04:13.652 --> 00:04:14.954
Everything about it is great.

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.089
So, speaking of old
movie theaters, so, this is,

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:18.991
I guess, Electric Palace.

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:20.326
Is that actually
what it's known as?

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.594
-Yeah.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:23.662
-And, so, you -- Tell me
about this beautiful old cinema.

00:04:23.662 --> 00:04:24.997
You're involved with it.

00:04:24.997 --> 00:04:27.032
-So, yeah, I've got a place
that's very near there,

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:29.568
and it's in this small town
called Harwich.

00:04:29.568 --> 00:04:31.337
And I've got a history
with the cinema.

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:35.241
I remember the first time I went
with my wife so many years ago,

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:36.676
there was only
the two of us in there,

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:38.377
and they were
playing "Moonstruck."

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:40.013
And and the girl
came up and said,

00:04:40.013 --> 00:04:41.480
"Oh, we've got a rule here.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.515
If there's not six people,
we don't screen it."

00:04:43.515 --> 00:04:46.719
And I, quick as a flash,
said, "Well, I'll pay for six."

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:47.753
And they said, "No,
if you're going to do that,

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:48.854
we're going to screen it."

00:04:48.854 --> 00:04:50.355
So I watched "Moonstruck"

00:04:50.355 --> 00:04:53.559
with my wife there on our own,
when we were very early dating.

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.594
But then they asked me
to become a patron,

00:04:55.594 --> 00:04:57.563
and I've helped them out a lot

00:04:57.563 --> 00:04:59.832
and I've sort of
helped them raise money,

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:01.968
got them funding
to keep the place going.

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:05.638
It was built in 1911.
It's still as it was then.

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:10.176
And after I got this job,
I got in contact with them

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:12.244
and I did
a "Maltese Falcon"/"Casablanca"

00:05:12.244 --> 00:05:15.548
double bill to a full cinema.

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:17.116
So, I like, in the end,
you were like,

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:18.383
"I'll pay for
four extra tickets."

00:05:18.383 --> 00:05:19.919
They're like, "Absolutely not."

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:21.954
And then it sounds like they've
just gotten thousands from you.

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:23.489
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:24.657
-You thought
they were being polite.

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.125
They were like,
"We got him on the hook."

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:28.928
-What goes around comes around.
-There you go. There you go.

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:32.298
South of France --
How is your French?

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:33.932
I mean, you have to do it a
little bit. I got nervous.

00:05:33.932 --> 00:05:35.134
My mom's a French teacher,

00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:37.303
and I got nervous about
saying "Monsieur Spade."

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:38.905
So how about you?

00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:42.107
-No. I mean,
I was asked at the beginning,

00:05:42.107 --> 00:05:43.509
"How's your French?

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:45.344
Is it good?"
I said, "It will be."

00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:48.847
-Oh, that's very nice.
That's a real good actor answer.

00:05:48.847 --> 00:05:51.417
-I tried to learn it
classically and realized

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:52.885
I was spending so much time

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:54.853
learning things
that I wasn't going to need.

00:05:54.853 --> 00:05:56.321
Like, I'm not going
to be tested.

00:05:56.321 --> 00:05:58.857
I don't need the grammar.
So I learned it phonetically.

00:05:58.857 --> 00:06:00.092
I learned it like
an accent and by sounds.

00:06:00.092 --> 00:06:01.694
-Oh, that's fantastic.
-Yeah.

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:02.928
-And do you feel like
it was better

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:05.898
when you left
the South of France?

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.099
-No.
-Okay.

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:08.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.068
-I like that now --
I like that, beforehand,

00:06:10.068 --> 00:06:12.404
you're like, "I'll know it
by the time I get on set."

00:06:12.404 --> 00:06:14.373
Now that the job's over,
you're like, "Nope."

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:16.776
That's when you find out a real
honest answer from an actor

00:06:16.776 --> 00:06:18.710
is when the job's over.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.413
Didn't speak it,
didn't get better.

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:22.214
I don't have to do it again.

00:06:22.214 --> 00:06:23.549
It's a really cool show,

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:24.917
and it's so nice
to have you back here.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:26.585
Thanks so much, Clive.
-It's a pleasure. Thank you.

00:06:26.585 --> 00:06:27.653
-Clive Owen, everybody.

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:30.222
"Monsieur Spade"
airs Sundays on AMC

00:06:30.222 --> 00:06:32.625
and streams on AMC+
and Acorn TV,

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:34.594
and "A Murder
at the End of the World --"

00:06:34.594 --> 00:06:36.228
also fantastic --
streaming on Hulu now.

00:06:36.228 --> 00:06:37.296
Stick around.

00:06:37.296 --> 00:06:38.598
We'll be right back with
Isla Fisher.

